Q: CodeIgniter view is neither showing query results, nor updating html table rows from AJAX requests A: <p>Your controller method is unconditionally calling your view file (which I presume contains all of the page elements in addition to your model result data). If you want to re-use the same controller method, then check if the call is from an AJAX request - in which case return the model payload as a JSON-encoded response and make it the client side's responsibility to empty and re-populate the table.</p>
<p>Your controller needs to assign a key name for the model payload which will be translated into a PHP variable in the view. Just because your model method is defined as <code>getDataExcelKK()</code> doesn't mean that there will magically be a <code>$getDataExcelKK</code> variable in your view.</p>
<p>Because your model is joining two tables by identically named columns, use a <code>USING</code> statement for simplicity.</p>
<p>Controller:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>public function filesListByStation()
{
    $station = $this-&gt;input-&gt;get('station'); 
    $data['filesWithDates'] = $this-&gt;M_dbstatmanagement-&gt;getFilesWithDates($station);
    if (!$this-&gt;input-&gt;is_ajax_request()) {
        $this-&gt;load-&gt;view('v_lhastat_admin', $data);
    } else {
        header('Content-Type: application/json');
        echo json_encode($data, JSON_PRETTY_PRINT);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Model (I don't recommend using uppercase letters in database identifiers; I prefer to distinguish SQL functions and keywords using ALL_CAPS):</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>public function getFilesWithDates(?string $station = null): array
{
    if ($string !== null) {
        $this-&gt;db
            -&gt;join('user u', 'ID_USER')
            -&gt;where('u.DOMISILI', $station);
    }

    return $this-&gt;db
        -&gt;select([
            'f.NAMA_FILE',
            &quot;DATE_FORMAT(f.TANGGAL_UPLOAD, '%d-%m-%Y %H:%i:%s') TANGGAL_UPLOAD&quot;
        ])
        -&gt;order_by('f.TANGGAL_UPLOAD', 'desc');
        -&gt;get('fileupload f')
        -&gt;result_array();
}
</code></pre>
<p>In your view template, iterate on <code>$filesWithDates</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-php-template prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;?php
foreach ($filesWithDates as $i =&gt; ['NAMA_FILE' =&gt; $fileName, 'TANGGAL_UPLOAD' =&gt; $uploadedOn]) { ?&gt;
    &lt;tr&gt;
        &lt;td&gt;&lt;?php echo $i + 1; ?&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
        &lt;td&gt;&lt;?php echo $fileName; ?&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
        &lt;td&gt;&lt;?php echo $uploadedOn; ?&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;?php } ?&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Your AJAX call (to <code>echo base_url('sms/filesListByStation');</code>) should clear the table rows, then rebuild the table by looping over the results in the <code>filesWithDates</code> property of the response payload. The <code>success</code> portion of your client-side scripting might resemble this:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>success: function(response) {
    var $tbody = $('#isi-data').empty();

    $.each(response.filesWithDates, function(index, row) {
        $('&lt;tr&gt;')
            .append($('&lt;td&gt;').text(index + 1))
            .append($('&lt;td&gt;').text(row.NAMA_FILE))
            .append($('&lt;td&gt;').text(row.TANGGAL_UPLOAD))
            .appendTo($tbody);
    });
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to remove or hide this &lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt; when resize the browser? A: <p>There are two ways to do your task using JS:</p>
<ol>
<li>resize the browser</li>
<li>using CSS</li>
</ol>
<p>The following demonstrates both, although in my opinion CSS is the better option:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
@media (max-width:991px){.cart span{display:none}}
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;cart&quot;&gt;&lt;i class=&quot;fa fa-cart&quot;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;&lt;span&gt;Cart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;p&gt;
&lt;script src=&quot;https://code.jquery.com/jquery-1.10.2.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script type=&quot;text/javascript&quot;&gt;
$(window).resize(function(){
var winW = $(window).width();
if(winW &lt;= 991){
$('.cart span').hide();
}else
{
$('.cart span').show();
}
});
&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
</code></pre>
